{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Though Elohim said: you shall not eat of any tree of the garden.</b> There is a great difficulty in this matter: for if the woman added of her own accord the command 'you shall not touch,' then she knew the truth — that HaShem had not commanded regarding touching — and if so, how was the woman seduced by these empty words to say, 'Just as there is no death in touching, so there is no death in eating'? At first glance it appears proper to explain that the woman did not hear the command from the mouth of the Gevurah but only from the mouth of her husband; and this is what it says (Genesis 2:16) ״וַיְצַו ה׳ אֱלֹהִים עַל הָאָדָם לֵאמֹר״ — what is ״לֵאמֹר״ ('to say')? Rather, that he should say to his wife as well that this eating is dangerous. For on this account it says ״עַל הָאָדָם״ ('upon the man') and it does not say ״אֶל הָאָדָם״ ('to the man') but rather ״עַל״ ('upon') — that is, concerning him, so as not to bring himself into danger. And the man saw with his intellect to make a fence and to add to the command by forbidding his wife even touching, so that she would not come to eating; and Chava thought that he told her everything from the mouth of the Gevurah. Therefore this error came to her, for the serpent found an opening to mislead her. And with this we have no need for Rashi's explanation, who explained that the reason the serpent did not come to Adam haRishon is because women's minds are lightly moved to seduction — for in any case this is no difficulty, since the serpent wanted to prove from the touching that there is no death in eating, and with Adam haRishon he could have proved nothing at all, for Adam knew the truth — that HaShem had not commanded regarding touching, and that he himself had added of his own mind." "And it appears proper to explain further, that Chava was in doubt whether the serpent's intention was for their benefit, so that they would be like Elohim, knowing good and evil, or perhaps his intention was so that the man would die and he himself would take Chava. And he spoke to Chava because women's minds are lightly moved to seduction, and he knew that she would surely give to her husband first. Therefore Chava said: by this I will test the mind of the serpent. And she added the command 'you shall not touch,' so that the whole exchange between them would revolve around touching — for with touching there is no reason she would let her husband precede her, as with eating. And when the serpent pushed Chava into the tree, then Chava said: now I know that the case is with the serpent — for if his intention had been that the man should die, if so why did he push me, and what would he gain by my death? Rather, surely as his words so it is — to become like Elohim — and therefore she gave place to his words. And see (Genesis 1:11) a precious explanation regarding the story of the serpent and Chava at the verse ״עֵץ עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי לְמִינוֹ״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "The cross-reference to Genesis 1:11 is given by the Kli Yakar himself; it points to his own commentary on that earlier verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:50.102600103Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}